[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m To find the least possible value of \( f(X) = AX + BX + CX + DX + EX \) for a point \( X \) in the plane of a convex pentagon \( ABCDE \) with given side lengths and angles, we can use the concept of the Fermat point and its properties. The Fermat point (or Torricelli point) of a triangle is the point that minimizes the sum of the distances to the vertices of the triangle. For an angle of \( 120^\circ \) at a vertex, the Fermat point is the point where the angles between the segments connecting it to the vertices are all \( 120^\circ \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m However, since \( \angle B = \angle E = 60^\circ \) and the pentagon is convex, we can use the fact that the problem can be approached by considering the geometry of the configuration and the properties of the distances.
