[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m To find the least possible value of \( f(X) = AX + BX + CX + DX + EX \), we need to consider the geometric properties of the pentagon and the specific angles given. The key insight is that the function \( f(X) \) is minimized when \( X \) is the Fermat point of the pentagon, which is a point that minimizes the sum of distances to the vertices of the pentagon. However, since \(\angle B = \angle E = 60^\circ\), we can simplify the problem by considering the symmetry and the given angles.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m First, we note that the triangles \(\triangle ABE\) and \(\triangle CDE\) are equilateral because \(\angle B = \angle E = 60^\circ\) and the sides \(AB = 14\), \(DE = 13\), \(BC = 7\), and \(CD = 24\). This means that the point \(X\) that minimizes \( f(X) \) will be the Fermat point of the pentagon, but due to the symmetry and the angles, it is easier to consider the point \(X\) that is the intersection of the diagonals of the equilateral triangles formed.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m The Fermat point of a triangle with all angles less than \(120^\circ\) is the point where the angles between the segments to the vertices of the triangle are all \(120^\circ\). However, in this case, we can use the fact that the sum of distances from the Fermat point to the vertices of the triangle is minimized, and this can be expressed in terms of the sides and the angles.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m 
