[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m To find the minimum value of \( f(X) = AX + BX + CX + DX + EX \), we need to locate a point \( X \) that minimizes the sum of the distances to the vertices of the pentagon \( ABCDE \). This point is known as the geometric median of the points \( A, B, C, D, E \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m First, we consider the geometry of the pentagon and the given angles. Since \( \angle B = \angle E = 60^\circ \), we can use the Law of Cosines in triangles \( ABC \) and \( CDE \) to find the lengths of \( AC \) and \( CE \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m For triangle \( ABC \) with \( AB = 14 \), \( BC = 7 \), and \( \angle B = 60^\circ \):
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m AC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2 - 2 \cdot AB \cdot BC \cdot \cos(60^\circ) = 14^2 + 7^2 - 2 \cdot 14 \cdot 7 \cdot \frac{1}{2} = 196 + 49 - 98 = 147
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m So, \( AC = \sqrt{147} = 7\sqrt{3} \).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1199197)[0m 
